# Our answer

**→ [`eeg-state-detection/README.md`](../eeg-state-detection/README.md)** is the write-up:
figures, tables, reproduction commands, and the limits.
Sponsor-record summary: [`FULFILLMENT_SPONSORS.md` → VoloRidge](../FULFILLMENT_SPONSORS.md#voloridge--public-data-challenge-a-simple-signal-and-the-nulls-that-test-it).

In person VoloRidge said they judge four things: how you handle data analysis, how you
extract signal from noise, how elegant the result is rather than how complex, and how you
validate. The write-up answers those four in order.

**The short version.** On [Shin et al. 2018 dataset A](https://doc.ml.tu-berlin.de/simultaneous_EEG_NIRS/),
28-channel EEG during an n-back task, five participants, fifteen sessions: the final model
is 84 numbers and a logistic regression. Seven more complex alternatives, from Riemannian
tangent space to Bayesian state-space models to zigzag persistent homology, were each tested
as paired comparisons on the same recordings, and every one lost or tied. The only addition
that survived is a one-parameter moving average.

Mean out-of-sample AUROC is 0.65 to 0.93, and **8 of 15 sessions beat a circular time-shift
null at p ≤ 0.05**.

The half we find more useful is the negative one. Brief evoked events turned out to be
undetectable by five methods across four participants. A zigzag persistent-homology detector
matched 6 of 36 events against a 2 of 36 baseline, which reads as a threefold improvement,
and sits at p = 0.13 against randomly thrown darts. **Without the null we would have shipped
it as a result.** That finding is why MemoryPalace captures forward on a persistence filter
rather than buffering backward for an instantaneous spike.
